#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""NCERT Class 3 Mathematics - Chapter 10 Part 1"""

slides_part1 = [{'id': 1, 'title': 'Classroom Decoration Measures', 'subtitle': 'Informal length measurement with paper streamers and party ribbons', 'speech': 'Welcome to our joyful mathematics laboratory where we explore how distances and physical objects are measured. When preparing an energetic classroom for a festive celebration, many decorative items need to be arranged carefully. Children often hang colourful paper streamers across the entrance door, drape shiny metallic ribbons along window frames, and stretch festive party bunting between opposite classroom walls. Before cutting any decoration material with scissors, we must first understand how long or tall each specific space happens to be. Without a formal wooden measuring ruler, we naturally begin with informal measurement methods. We might use our own footsteps to pace the walking distance across the tiled floor, use our outstretched arms to gauge the horizontal width of a window, or use cut pieces of string to match the vertical height of the doorway. These non-standard measuring tools help us compare different lengths directly in our immediate environment. We can clearly observe that the entrance door is much taller than a study table, while a pencil is significantly shorter than a party ribbon. By testing and comparing lengths using simple everyday cords, we build a clear visual understanding of distance, height, and perimeter boundaries. Every festive decoration project becomes an exciting practical exercise in discovering spatial relationships and comparative sizes.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which informal tool is best suited for directly comparing the height of a doorway without a metal measuring tape?', 'options': ['A cut piece of paper string', 'A single grain of rice', 'A heavy wooden desk', 'A round balloon'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of a flexible material that can stretch straight along the tall frame.', 'explanation': 'A long flexible string can be held straight against the doorway to capture its exact height directly.'}, {'question': 'What is the primary purpose of comparing lengths using non-standard cords and ribbons?', 'options': ['To build intuitive understanding of comparative length and distance', 'To make the classroom look plain and empty', 'To weigh heavy furniture items', 'To count how many windows exist in the school'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Informal measurements help develop early spatial and size relationships.', 'explanation': 'Comparing lengths using cords allows learners to intuitively judge longer, shorter, and equal distances before learning standard units.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'title': 'Comparing Hand Spans', 'subtitle': 'Discovering why non-standard handspan units vary across different individuals', 'speech': 'A traditional informal way to measure everyday furniture is the familiar human handspan. A handspan is the direct span measured from the tip of the thumb to the tip of the little finger when your palm and fingers are stretched out as wide as possible. If two learners measure the exact same wooden study table in the classroom, an intriguing mathematical puzzle quickly appears. One learner might find that the long tabletop measures eight handspans across, while a classroom companion measuring the identical edge counts ten handspans. Why does the number of handspans change if the sturdy wooden table itself has not expanded or shrunk at all? The answer lies in the natural physical size differences of individual hands. A person with larger hands covers more distance with each individual span, so fewer total spans are needed to travel across the tabletop. A person with smaller hands requires more spans to cover that identical physical distance. Because hand sizes vary widely from child to child, handspans cannot serve as a universally dependable unit of measurement. However, practicing with handspans helps us understand that measurement is essentially counting how many small units fit into a larger length. This fundamental realization lays the foundation for creating uniform standard units.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If a tabletop measures 8 large handspans, how many smaller handspans will likely be needed for the same table?', 'options': ['More than 8 handspans', 'Fewer than 8 handspans', 'Exactly 2 handspans', 'Zero handspans'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Smaller units cover less distance per step, so more steps are required.', 'explanation': 'Because each small handspan covers a shorter distance, more spans are needed to cover the entire table edge.'}, {'question': 'What is a handspan defined as?', 'options': ['Distance from thumb tip to little finger tip when fully outstretched', 'Length of the elbow to the wrist', 'Distance between two adjacent footsteps', 'Total width of the palm closed tightly'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think of spreading your hand as wide as possible on the desk.', 'explanation': 'A handspan is the maximum outstretched distance between the tip of the thumb and the tip of the little finger.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'title': 'Display Board Borders', 'subtitle': 'Calculating the total boundary perimeter using uniform decorative paper strings', 'speech': 'When decorating the classroom green display board, colourful paper ribbons are often pasted neatly along all four outer edges. This outer boundary enclosing a flat two-dimensional surface is known in geometry as the perimeter. Suppose we have short decorative paper strings that are each cut to the exact same length. To decorate the entire top border of the board, we place these paper strings end to end in a straight line without leaving any gaps or overlapping them. If the top edge requires six paper strings and the opposite bottom edge also requires six paper strings, that gives twelve strings for the two horizontal sides. Next, we measure the vertical sides. If each vertical side edge requires four paper strings, the two vertical borders together need eight strings. Adding all four borders together, six plus six plus four plus four equals twenty strings in total. Notice how measuring an entire perimeter involves adding the lengths of all outer boundary lines systematically. We also discover that a single long continuous string could cover the entire border if its length matched twenty short decorative segments joined together. This hands-on activity demonstrates how repeated unit lengths combine to measure complete rectangular boundaries accurately.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A rectangular board takes 5 paper strings across the top and 3 along the side. What is its total border perimeter in strings?', 'options': ['16 strings (5 + 5 + 3 + 3)', '8 strings (5 + 3)', '15 strings (5 × 3)', '10 strings (5 + 5)'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Remember a rectangle has two equal horizontal sides and two equal vertical sides.', 'explanation': 'The total boundary includes both top and bottom edges (5 + 5 = 10) plus both vertical sides (3 + 3 = 6), giving 16 strings.'}, {'question': 'When placing measuring strings along a border, what important rule must be followed?', 'options': ['Place them end-to-end with no gaps and no overlapping', 'Overlap each string by half its length', 'Leave large gaps between successive strings', 'Roll each string into a tight round ball'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Accurate linear measurement requires continuous, non-overlapping unit placement.', 'explanation': 'Accurate measurement requires units to be laid consecutively end-to-end without leaving gaps or overlapping.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'title': 'Symmetrical Moustache Halves', 'subtitle': 'Exploring mirror reflection symmetry and equal opposite lengths on party masks', 'speech': 'During class party preparations, children frequently design amusing costume masks featuring painted faces and whimsical curled moustaches. When crafting an attractive balanced mask, a key geometric property comes into play called bilateral mirror symmetry. If one half of a curled moustache is drawn on the left side of the mask nose, the matching right half must mirror it perfectly in shape and size. That means the right half must have the exact same length, the same curvature, and point outwards in the opposite balanced direction. If the left side measures ten centimetres from the central nose line to its outer tip, the right side must also measure ten centimetres. If one side were drawn fifteen centimetres long while the other remained only five centimetres, the mask would look visibly uneven, awkward, and lopsided. Symmetry teaches us that equal distance segments can extend outward in opposite directions from a central line of balance. We see similar symmetry in our own human faces, where both eyebrows, eyes, and ears are positioned at matching distances from the central facial axis. Practicing symmetric drawing reinforces our appreciation of equal length segments and spatial harmony in both creative art and mathematical geometry.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If the left curl of a party mask moustache is 8 centimetres long, how long must the symmetrical right curl be?', 'options': ['Exactly 8 centimetres', '16 centimetres', '4 centimetres', '12 centimetres'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Symmetry means both halves are identical in length and shape.', 'explanation': 'Bilateral symmetry requires both matching halves to have identical length, so the right curl must also be 8 cm.'}, {'question': 'What is the central reference line called that divides a symmetrical figure into two matching halves?', 'options': ['Line of symmetry', 'Curved border loop', 'Diagonal corner cut', 'Outer perimeter track'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'It is the imaginary line across which reflections match.', 'explanation': 'The line of symmetry is the balance line across which both halves of a figure mirror each other perfectly.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'title': 'Doorway Passage Turning', 'subtitle': 'Solving spatial clearance problems by rotating 2D dimensions through narrow doorways', 'speech': 'An entertaining spatial puzzle occurs when students attempt to carry a large rectangular activity table out through the classroom entrance. When the table is carried flat in its normal upright sitting position, its wide tabletop bumps firmly against both doorposts and cannot pass. The learners use a piece of string to measure the width of the open doorway and compare it carefully to the table dimensions. They discover that the table has two distinct horizontal measurements: its long length and its significantly narrower breadth. While the long length of the table is clearly wider than the open doorway, the table breadth or height is significantly narrower than the door frame. By turning the table sideways on its side or tilting it at a gentle angle, the narrower dimension aligns with the doorway opening. Now, the entire table glides smoothly through the entrance without touching the doorposts at all. This practical challenge reveals that three-dimensional physical objects have multiple dimensional aspects. Solving real-world clearance problems requires analyzing which specific dimension is being tested against an opening and rotating the object intelligently to find its most accommodating orientation for passage. This clever geometric adjustment turns an impossible obstruction into a very simple, manageable, and satisfying maneuvering victory for all learners.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Why could the table pass through the narrow door after being turned on its side?', 'options': ['Its narrower breadth or thickness was smaller than the door width', 'The doorway magically grew wider', 'The table shrank in weight', 'The table legs were sawed off'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about which dimension faces the opening after turning.', 'explanation': 'Turning the table aligned its narrower dimension (breadth/height) with the door opening, allowing it to pass.'}, {'question': 'What does a piece of measuring string help verify before moving large furniture?', 'options': ["Whether the object's dimensions are smaller than the doorway opening", 'What color paint was used on the door', 'How many people can sit at the table', 'The temperature inside the classroom'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'String captures physical length for direct comparison without moving heavy items.', 'explanation': "Using a string allows quick physical comparison of the table's dimensions against the door clearance."}]}, {'id': 6, 'title': 'Under Bridge Clearance', 'subtitle': 'Testing height and width boundaries beneath constructed classroom toy bridges', 'speech': 'During free play activities, children often build toy bridges across the classroom floor using cardboard cartons, stacked textbooks, and solid wooden building blocks. A toy bridge creates an elevated horizontal span with an open tunnel underneath. The empty space below the bridge has two critical boundary measurements: the vertical clearance height from floor to span, and the horizontal clearance width between the supporting side pillars. Students test different toy vehicles to predict which ones can pass safely underneath without crashing into the bridge structure. A flat toy racing car easily speeds through because its height is far lower than the bridge clearance. However, a tall toy double-decker bus or a cargo truck with a tall crane gets firmly stuck at the archway because its physical height exceeds the vertical clearance. If the bridge pillars are built too close together, even a low car cannot enter if its body is wider than the pillar gap. Designing effective tunnels and bridges teaches us that physical passage requires checking both height and width boundaries simultaneously against the moving object dimensions before attempting movement. Observing which toy vehicles glide smoothly and which ones collide helps young builders develop keen spatial judgment, structural foresight, and clearance estimation skills.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A toy bridge has a vertical clearance of 12 cm. Which toy vehicle can pass safely underneath?', 'options': ['A toy sports car that is 5 cm tall', 'A toy crane truck that is 15 cm tall', 'A double-decker bus that is 14 cm tall', 'A stuffed giraffe that is 18 cm tall'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': "To pass underneath, the object's height must be strictly less than the clearance height.", 'explanation': 'The 5 cm sports car is lower than the 12 cm clearance (5 cm < 12 cm), so it passes through easily.'}, {'question': 'What two dimensions determine whether an object fits through a tunnel opening?', 'options': ['Height and width of the opening', 'Color and paint gloss', 'Weight and price of the materials', 'Time of day and temperature'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Think about the vertical and horizontal limits of the opening.', 'explanation': 'Height and width define the 2D cross-sectional clearance needed for an object to pass without collision.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'title': 'Body Proportion Truths', 'subtitle': 'Investigating fascinating anatomical proportions and natural measurement relationships', 'speech': "While designing costumes for classroom plays, children often measure each other's body parts and test fascinating anatomical rules of thumb. Ancient artists and modern scientists have noted that the human body contains several remarkably consistent proportional relationships. For instance, if you carefully measure the length of your forearm from the inner elbow crease to the wrist, you will discover that it is almost identical to the length of your own foot from heel to big toe. Another remarkable proportion is that when you stretch both arms wide to the sides, your total arm span from fingertip to fingertip is approximately equal to your total standing height. Furthermore, wrapping a flexible paper strip around your forehead shows that head circumference is roughly three handspans. Although these body proportions are remarkably consistent among healthy growing children, they are not rigid mathematical equations for every single person. Individuals have natural variations in limb lengths, foot sizes, and skeletal build. Exploring these natural ratios helps us appreciate how early humans used their own bodies as the very first portable measuring tools for crafting clothing, constructing shelters, and trading goods. By observing how body parts relate proportionally, learners gain a deep personal intuition for organic harmony and balance.", 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'According to common human body proportions, which body part has approximately the same length as your foot?', 'options': ['Your forearm (elbow to wrist)', 'Your little finger', 'Your entire leg', 'Your neck height'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Try placing your shoe sole along your inner forearm to test this natural rule.', 'explanation': 'In human body proportions, the forearm length from inner elbow to wrist closely matches foot length.'}, {'question': 'Approximately what measurement matches your total standing height when measured with your body?', 'options': ['Your outstretched arm span from fingertip to fingertip', 'Two handspans of your palm', 'The length of your thumb nail', 'Half the length of your shoe'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Vitruvian proportions link standing height to horizontal arm spread.', 'explanation': "A person's total outstretched arm span is approximately equal to their total standing height."}]}, {'id': 8, 'title': 'Measuring Scale Evolution', 'subtitle': 'The historical progression from body units to uniform standard metre rods', 'speech': 'As human societies grew and engaged in active commerce across neighbouring towns, relying solely on human body parts created continuous confusion and endless disagreements. Imagine buying cloth from a merchant with very short arms, only to receive significantly less fabric than when buying from someone with longer arms. To eliminate unfair differences in trade, human communities gradually evolved much more reliable measuring devices. First, people created standardized sticks and cut paper strips of mutually agreed length. Eventually, scientists, mathematicians, and craftsmen established universal standard units of measurement that remain completely identical everywhere in the world. The primary standard unit of length in the international metric system is the metre, abbreviated with the small letter m. Today, cloth merchants and professional tailors rely on a sturdy metal or polished wooden metre rod to measure fabrics with absolute accuracy. Whether a metre is measured in Delhi, Tokyo, London, or New York, one metre is always exactly the same length. Standard units ensure fairness in commerce, precision in architecture, and reliable communication in science. Moving from informal body spans to the standard metre represents a monumental leap in human civilization and mathematical precision. Standard units connect the entire world together under a shared, trusted mathematical language of measurement.', 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the primary international standard unit of length introduced to replace informal body units?', 'options': ['The metre (m)', 'The handspan', 'The shoeprint', 'The thumb knuckle'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'It is the standard length marked on rigid rods used by cloth merchants.', 'explanation': 'The metre (abbreviated as m) is the worldwide standard base unit of length.'}, {'question': 'Why did merchants replace body part measurements with standardized metal metre rods?', 'options': ['To guarantee fair, identical, and reproducible lengths everywhere', 'Because metal rods are colorful and easy to bend', 'To make measuring take twice as much time', 'Because body parts never change size'], 'answer': 0, 'hint': 'Uniform standards prevent disputes caused by varying arm sizes.', 'explanation': 'Standardized metre rods ensure that lengths remain fair, consistent, and trusted across all people and locations.'}]}]

